"""Render bounded batch06 reports from reviewed raw figures and numeric receipts."""
import csv,hashlib,json
from pathlib import Path
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
Q=list(csv.DictReader((B/'queue.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8')))
SECT={x['tic']:x for x in json.loads((B/'current-sector-availability.json').read_text())}
STATUS={x['tic']:x for x in list(csv.DictReader((B.parent/'shared-catalogues'/'TARGET-STATUS.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8')))}
DATA={
'288157030':('REFUTED','The retained s87 deficit is a downward discontinuity immediately after the 3676.44350-3676.65646 BTJD interruption, followed by gradual recovery rather than an ingress/egress pair. In adjacent quality-zero 0.2-d windows SAP falls 1.02010 to 0.97401 of the sector median while SAP_BKG rises 1727.65 to 6100.88. Earlier s6 and s33 show broad trends, not a same-kind recurring eclipse.','The s87 step has no measured eclipse midpoint; its background response makes the selected flag instrumental. Current TESSCut footprint lists new s108/s109 coverage, not analyzed here because the selected feature is positively refuted.','Review s108/s109 raw target pixels if an independent future eclipse claim arises.'),
'41975422':('REFUTED','The two dramatic s69 descents near 3193.3 and 3206.55 BTJD occur at spacecraft orbit/sector interruptions, with flagged lowest cadences and sudden recovery after the first gap. SAP_BKG rises 965.6 to 44960 over the first decline and 43.84 to 35350 over the second while SAP changes 1.004 to 0.942 and 1.001 to 0.952. Eight same-CCD comparison targets lack a matching drop: the evidence is target-specific background/aperture corruption, not a detector-wide veto.','There are no reliable eclipse centers or cycle count from these two interruption-edge descents. Current TESSCut lists s104-s107 in addition to cached sectors; those are not needed to establish the retained s69 feature as instrumental.','Inspect current-sector target pixels only if an independent eclipse indication appears.'),
'100938468':('REFUTED','The retained s88 deficit is an immediate step after the 3703.89294-3704.10359 BTJD interruption, then slow recovery. Adjacent quality-zero SAP medians change 0.97150 to 0.95710 while SAP_BKG changes -177.17 to 214.995. The other four cached sectors contain broad trends and no matching eclipse.','The s88 shape is a gap-associated background step, without measured eclipse ingress, center or egress.','Seek a clean future raw eclipse before any physical-period claim.'),
'198279430':('REFUTED','The s17 retained dip lies on a monotonic rising shoulder entering an interruption. Brightening flags in s84 near 3586 and 3599 BTJD coincide with SAP_BKG jumps -331.75 to 3790.5 and -407.07 to 5254.4 while SAP rises 0.99897 to 1.011 and 0.99838 to 1.0203. The extensive raw series contains background/step features, not a repeated eclipse morphology.','Bright features can be variable or instrumental; the stated background correlation is positive contrary evidence for these retained eclipse flags, not a proof that the star has no intrinsic variability.','Revisit only with independently extracted stable-background pixel photometry.'),
'417309915':('REFUTED','The s24 selected dip overlaps the 1968.31957-1969.27791 BTJD interruption. Its quality-zero core SAP median 0.99538 matches the preceding 0.99408; the apparent dip was made by using the following brightening (post median 1.05563) as baseline. Target-centered s24 TESSCut 1x1 and 3x3 pixels independently show flat flux before the interruption and an enormous positive rise just afterward, peaking around 1970.7. There is no negative eclipse feature to time in the selected window.','The brightening physics is unsettled; the selected dip is a baseline artifact. The pixel extraction is of the same TESS photons and not another observing occasion.','If the brightenings matter independently, compare calibrated pixel background across spacecraft orbits.'),
'51255152':('REFUTED','The two s99 retained dip windows at 4051.87 and 4068.53 BTJD sample very large, slowly changing raw flux excursions. At the latter, pre/core/post quality-zero QLP SAP medians are 0.971/0.859/0.855. Target-centered TESSCut pixels independently show a slow decline before the 4067.19-4067.41 spacecraft gap, a downward step across it, then a broad rise after 4071 into much stronger brightening. The dip window is the low plateau of this step/ramp sequence, with no localized ingress or egress.','The selected s99 deficit is not a localized eclipse; strong variability or extraction instability deserves separate investigation. The TESSCut extraction is of the same photons.','Use calibrated independent aperture photometry if seeking the origin of the large excursions.'),
'280963520':('PLAUSIBLE','The s9 short dips at 1552.681, 1553.431 and 1554.910 BTJD are separated by approximately two and four cycles of a 0.37530-d raw-flux periodogram peak. A short-period phase fold shows a coherent depression in raw SAP and the QLP corrected variant, so these cannot be dismissed as isolated noise or artifact. The s62 brightening is a different morphology and is not counted as an eclipse.','Short-period variability, potentially a short-period eclipsing by-catch, has no secure source attribution or established orbital/eclipse kind. A raw s9 TESSCut pixel extraction is dominated by a sawtooth trend and does not validate the QLP depression. It does not establish an eclipse on the 32.95-d Gaia spectroscopic period. New s99 coverage exists, but that official TESSCut 5x5 target footprint has effectively zero flux and is not a sensitive recurrence test.','Obtain calibrated target-pixel photometry to test 0.37530/0.75060-d aliases and locate the variable source.'),
'164669150':('REFUTED','Retained s53/s55/s74/s75/s80/s82 dips and brightenings repeatedly straddle interruptions with discontinuous SAP steps. The s53 and s55 apparent dips occur after 0.92-d gaps on a smooth broad ramp; s74/s75/s80 show step changes across 0.21-d orbit gaps and broad post-gap brightenings. They are not bounded eclipse ingress/egress pairs.','Multiple sectors make the gap pattern recur, but they do not supply repeated same-kind eclipses. Background and baseline behavior leave the origin of broad variability open.','Inspect independent target-pixel photometry only if a stable eclipse ephemeris emerges.'),
'405542204':('PLAUSIBLE','The s25/s26 QLP SAP windows contain low-amplitude multi-day depressions, and the s25 5x5 TESSCut pixel series confirms broad structure with a deeper decline near the sector end around 2009 BTJD. The s25 retained 2006.41 window lies on a smooth declining shoulder; s26 has two broad 2015/2028 windows with different shapes. These are not measured eclipse centers, but the independent target-centered extraction prevents dismissing the s25 depression as a QLP-only false positive. The s79 bright flag is a separate brief spike.','A long eclipse remains possible. No complete same-kind repeated ingress/egress pair, secure cycle count, source attribution or geometry has been established; the raw pixel extraction shares TESS photons.','Obtain calibrated target-centered pixel photometry across the s25/s26 boundary and a future predicted interval to establish whether a localized eclipse repeats.'),
'180177949':('REFUTED','Quality-zero raw SAP shows a persistent approximately 1.03-d sinusoidal wave in s11, s37, s64, s99, s100 and s101; independent per-sector frequency searches gave finite-grid peaks at 1.0323, 1.0334, 1.0315, 1.0316, 1.0305 and 1.0297 d. The retained broad dips and brightenings sample this wave, with no localized long-period eclipse shape.','The short-period modulation is real but its physical mechanism and relation to the Gaia SB2 are not settled. The selected long-period eclipse flag is refuted, not all possible eclipses.','Classify the short-period variable separately if of interest.'),
}
out=[]
for q in Q:
 tic=q['tic'];ver,evidence,lim,nxt=DATA[tic];o=B/tic
 n=json.loads((o/'initial-numerics.json').read_text());st=json.loads((o/'step-context.json').read_text());cat=STATUS[tic];avail=SECT[tic]
 lines=[f'# {q["sample_id"]} - TIC {tic}', '',f'Physical verdict: **{ver}**. Bounded review: **COMPLETE**. Catalogue novelty: **UNKNOWN**.', '',f'Selection: {q["sample_stratum"]}, Gaia DR3 {q["gaia_dr3"]}, Gaia NSS {q["nss_type"]} period {q["gaia_period_days"]} d. This is a selection orbit, not a measured eclipse period.', '', '## Raw evidence','', evidence,'', '## Coverage, timing and attribution','',f'{len(n["coverage"])} verified cached QLP sectors; coverage and retained-window numbers are in `initial-numerics.json` and `step-context.json`. `predicted-windows.json` lists actual cadence and sideband samples at Gaia-period and half-phase predictions; these are not clean nulls without raw context. Screening windows were predictions; no eclipse period, primary epoch or depth was measured. {lim}', '', 'The shared catalogue matrix records four unavailable VizieR gates (VSX, ASAS-SN and Kostov 2025 tables 3/4). Exact nonmatches in available primary tables and Gaia do not establish novelty. The shared 120-arcsec Gaia neighbor cone is available in `../../shared-catalogues/gaia-neighbors.json`; no neighbor exclusion or Gaia-orbit geometry inference is needed to refute the selected non-eclipse feature.', '',f'Official TESSCut sector query: {avail.get("request_url")}; newer sectors than cached QLP: {avail.get("newer_than_cached")}. Query response SHA-256: {avail.get("response_sha256")}.', '', '## Limits and next check','',nxt,'', '## Sources','',f'Bulk detector packet: `{n["source_bulk"]}` (SHA-256 {n["source_bulk_sha256"]}). Each QLP FITS path and verified SHA-256 is listed in `initial-numerics.json`. Plots: `raw-flags.png` and `raw-overview.png`. Scripts: `inspect_raw.py`, `check_steps.py`, `predicted_windows.py`, `current_sectors.py`, `write_reports.py`. The official cutout is independent pixel extraction of the same photons if used; it is not an independent observing occasion.']
 if tic in ('280963520','405542204'):
  lines[lines.index('The shared catalogue matrix records four unavailable VizieR gates (VSX, ASAS-SN and Kostov 2025 tables 3/4). Exact nonmatches in available primary tables and Gaia do not establish novelty. The shared 120-arcsec Gaia neighbor cone is available in `../../shared-catalogues/gaia-neighbors.json`; no neighbor exclusion or Gaia-orbit geometry inference is needed to refute the selected non-eclipse feature.')]='The shared catalogue matrix records four unavailable VizieR gates (VSX, ASAS-SN and Kostov 2025 tables 3/4). Exact nonmatches in available primary tables and Gaia do not establish novelty. The shared 120-arcsec Gaia neighbor cone is in `../../shared-catalogues/gaia-neighbors.json`; source attribution and orbital geometry remain unresolved.'
 (o/'REPORT.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
 if tic=='280963520':
  with (o/'REPORT.md').open('a',encoding='utf-8') as f:
   f.write('\n## Additional short-period checks\n\nThe helper period search and s99 WCS/pixel audit are in `../helper-T057-numerics.json` and `../helper-T057-s99.json`, with scripts and hashes. The s99 target projects to pixel (2.122, 2.194), whose median flux is about -0.0004; columns 1-4 are near zero. This cutout cannot provide a null. `qlp-variant-fold.png` compares SAP and KSPSAP; the latter is another reduction within QLP of the same s9 photons. A separate s9 TESSCut extraction (`s09-pixels.json`, `s09-pixel-fold.png`) has real target flux but a strong raw sawtooth; it does not independently validate the short-period depression.\n')
 if tic in ('417309915','51255152','405542204'):
  with (o/'REPORT.md').open('a',encoding='utf-8') as f:
   f.write('\n## Target-centered pixel check\n\n`deciding-pixels.json` records the WCS position, positive target-pixel flux and cutout SHA-256. `deciding-pixels.png` shows 1x1, 3x3 and 5x5 raw cutout sums around the deciding window. Extraction and plot reproduce with `../inspect_deciders.py`.\n')
 out.append(dict(sample_id=q['sample_id'],tic=tic,sample_stratum=q['sample_stratum'],physical_verdict=ver,full_review_completion='COMPLETE',novelty_status='UNKNOWN',period_days='',period_error_days='',primary_epoch_btjd='',depth_ppt='',limitations=lim,next_check=nxt,report=str(o/'REPORT.md')))
with (B/'RESULTS.csv').open('w',newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f:
 w=csv.DictWriter(f,fieldnames=list(out[0]));w.writeheader();w.writerows(out)
